# AudD CLI (audd)

Use `audd` to identify music and work with an AudD account. Its output is JSON
when piped, so read stdout as JSON and errors from stderr.

## Which command

- One file or URL: `audd recognize <file|url>`. The standard endpoint analyzes
  up to the first 12 seconds; add `--at 1:30` to send a clip from another point.
- Every song in a long recording (a mix, a show, a video): `audd recognize <file>
  --enterprise --limit N`. Billed per 12-second chunk; add `--tracklist` for
  tracks with start and end times.
- A folder, glob, or list of files: `audd recognize <dir> --max-files N`. One
  JSON line per file, then a summary line. Resume with `audd jobs resume <id>`.
- The microphone: `audd listen`.
- Streams: `audd streams list`, `audd streams add <url> --id N`,
  `audd now-playing --once`, `audd streams history --since 24h`,
  `audd streams report --by artist`. A background recorder saves every stream
  result locally, so history and reports are complete.
- Custom catalog upload: `audd catalog add <file> --id N` (billed, never retried).
- Account: `audd usage`, `audd account`, `audd billing plans`.
- Docs: `audd docs api`, `audd docs streams`, `audd docs enterprise`, `audd docs cli`.
- Anything else: `audd api <method> key=value...`, and `audd commands --json`
  for every command and flag.

## Before spending requests

- Run billable work with `--dry-run` first and read the plan (files, estimated
  requests).
- Always pass explicit bounds: `--limit N` with `--enterprise`, `--max-files N`
  for folders and lists, and `--max-requests N` to cap a whole run. Never use
  `none` unless the user asked for no limit.
- Without a terminal, confirmations need `--yes`. Only add it after the user
  agreed to the plan.
- Results are cached by file contents, so repeating a recognition is free.

## Reading results

- No match is not an error: `"result": null` and exit code 0.
- Errors on stderr are `{"schema_version":1,"error":{"code","api_code","message","hint","retryable"}}`.
  `hint` is usually the command that fixes it.
- Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 unexpected, 2 invalid arguments or missing tool, 3 auth,
  4 quota or plan, 5 network or server, 6 a missing bound or confirmation
  (`--limit`, `--max-files`, `--yes`, `--max-requests`) or an unfinished job
  for the same files (error code `resume_available`: rerun with `--resume` to
  continue it or `--new` to start over), 7 batch partly failed,
  8 `usage --check` threshold, 130 stopped with Ctrl-C (resume a batch with
  `audd jobs resume ID`).
- `--fields a,b` trims the output; `--format jsonl|csv` changes it.

## Tokens and sign-in

- Never print or log the API token. `audd token show` masks it; do not add
  `--reveal`.
- If there is no token, ask the user to set `AUDD_API_TOKEN`, or run
  `audd login` yourself: without a terminal it prints a `login_pending` JSON
  line. Relay `verification_uri_complete` and `user_code` to the user (they
  open the page on any device, check the code, and approve) and keep the
  command running until it prints the result. Account commands need
  `audd login`.
- Payment commands (`audd billing subscribe|renew|buy`) only print a link for
  the user to open; never open it on their behalf.
